# Farm Data Use Agreement

**Project Title:** [Project Title]  
**Research Institution:** [University / Research Organization]  
**Principal Investigator:** [Name]  
**Research Team Contact:** [Email / Phone]  
**Farm / Data Provider:** [Farm Name / Farmer Name]  
**Effective Date:** [Date]

---

## 1. Purpose

This agreement describes how farm data provided by the farmer or farm organization will be collected, stored, analyzed, used, shared, and protected for research purposes.

The purpose of the research is to improve knowledge related to agricultural production, animal science, nutrition, farm management, sustainability, data science, or other approved research objectives.

The research team will use the data only for purposes described in this agreement and will take reasonable measures to protect the privacy and confidentiality of the farmer and the farm.

---

## 2. Data Covered by This Agreement

The farmer may provide one or more of the following types of data:

- Animal identification and demographic information
- Herd or flock size
- Breed, age, sex, and production stage
- Animal growth and body weight data
- Milk, egg, meat, or other production data
- Feed intake and feeding records
- Feed ingredients and diet composition
- Nutrient composition and supplementation information
- Animal health and treatment records
- Reproduction records
- Housing and farm-management information
- Environmental data, including temperature, humidity, and other production conditions
- Sensor, equipment, or automated monitoring data
- Economic or production-efficiency data, when specifically agreed
- Other research-related information: [Describe]

Only data necessary for the approved research objectives will be collected and used.

---

## 3. Data Ownership and Permission to Use

The farmer retains ownership of the original farm data unless otherwise specified in a separate written agreement.

By entering into this agreement, the farmer grants the research team permission to use the agreed data for:

- Scientific research and analysis
- Statistical modeling
- Development and evaluation of predictive models
- Machine-learning and artificial-intelligence research, when applicable
- Comparison or integration with other approved research datasets
- Academic publications
- Scientific presentations
- Research reports
- Development of research methods, tools, or analytical frameworks

The data will not be sold to third parties.

Commercial use, licensing, or use outside the agreed research scope will require additional written permission unless otherwise specified in this agreement.

---

## 4. Privacy and Confidentiality

The research team will take reasonable measures to protect the identity of the farmer and farm.

Whenever possible:

- Farmer names and farm names will be removed from analytical datasets.
- Personal contact information will be stored separately from research data.
- Farm addresses and precise geographic information will not be disclosed unless necessary for the research and specifically approved.
- Research datasets will use coded or de-identified farm identifiers.
- Results will normally be reported in aggregated or de-identified form.
- Individual farms will not be identified in publications, presentations, or reports without written permission.

Access to identifiable farm information will be limited to authorized members of the research team.

---

## 5. Data Storage and Security

Research data will be stored using secure systems approved by the research institution whenever possible.

Data may be stored on:

- Secure institutional servers
- Approved cloud-computing platforms
- Restricted research databases
- Encrypted storage systems
- Other approved research infrastructure

Access to the data will be limited according to the sensitivity of the information.

The research team will use reasonable safeguards such as:

- Password-protected accounts
- Access controls
- Encryption when appropriate
- Secure backups
- Controlled data-transfer procedures
- Separation of identifying information from analytical data

If a data-security incident occurs that may affect confidential farm information, the research team will follow applicable institutional procedures.

---

## 6. FAIR Data Principles

The research team will manage the research data according to the FAIR principles where appropriate.

FAIR means that data should be **Findable, Accessible, Interoperable, and Reusable**.

FAIR data management does not require confidential farm data to be publicly available.

### 6.1 Findable

To improve the organization and traceability of the data:

- Data files will use clear and consistent names.
- Metadata will describe the source, content, and structure of the dataset.
- Variables and measurement units will be documented.
- Data dictionaries will be created when appropriate.
- Dataset versions and major data-processing steps will be recorded.
- Persistent or internal identifiers may be assigned when appropriate.

These practices help researchers locate, understand, and manage the data correctly.

### 6.2 Accessible

Access to farm data will depend on the sensitivity of the information and the permissions granted under this agreement.

- Identifiable farm data will not automatically be made publicly available.
- Access may be restricted to approved researchers or collaborators.
- De-identified or aggregated data may be shared when permitted.
- Data-access restrictions will be documented.
- Metadata may remain available even when access to the underlying dataset is restricted.

Any sharing of confidential data must follow the conditions established in this agreement and applicable institutional policies.

### 6.3 Interoperable

Whenever practical, data will be organized using commonly accepted formats and standards.

This may include:

- Standard file formats such as CSV
- Consistent date formats
- Standard measurement units
- Clearly defined variable names
- Documented category codes
- Standard terminology where available
- Clear definitions for missing or unavailable data

These practices make it easier to combine, compare, and analyze data across different software platforms or research projects.

### 6.4 Reusable

To support responsible future use and reproducibility:

- The source of the data will be documented.
- Data-cleaning procedures will be recorded.
- Variable transformations will be documented.
- Analytical methods will be described.
- Research code may be retained when appropriate.
- Data limitations will be documented.
- Any future reuse of the farm data will follow the permissions established in this agreement.

Reuse of identifiable or confidential farm data outside the original research scope may require additional permission.

---

## 7. Data Processing and Analysis

The research team may clean, organize, standardize, transform, combine, and analyze the farm data for approved research purposes.

Analyses may include:

- Descriptive statistics
- Trend analysis
- Group comparisons
- Regression analysis
- Data visualization
- Predictive modeling
- Machine learning
- Artificial intelligence methods
- Optimization models
- Other appropriate statistical or computational methods

The research team will make reasonable efforts to use scientifically appropriate methods and report uncertainty and limitations.

---

## 8. Use of Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning

When appropriate, farm data may be used to develop, train, validate, or evaluate statistical, machine-learning, or artificial-intelligence models.

Possible research applications may include:

- Prediction of animal performance
- Prediction of nutrient requirements
- Prediction of feed efficiency
- Prediction of animal health outcomes
- Prediction of food or product quality
- Evaluation of environmental or management factors
- Development of farm decision-support tools

Where possible, the research team will document:

- The variables used in the model
- Data-preprocessing procedures
- Training and validation methods
- Model performance
- Important limitations
- Sources of uncertainty

Artificial-intelligence or predictive-model outputs will be treated as research results and should not automatically be considered professional veterinary, financial, nutritional, or management advice.

---

## 9. Data Sharing With Research Collaborators

Farm data may be shared with approved research collaborators when such sharing is necessary for the research.

Collaborators receiving confidential or identifiable data must follow appropriate confidentiality and data-protection requirements.

Whenever possible, collaborators will receive:

- De-identified data
- Coded data
- Aggregated data
- Only the minimum data necessary for their role in the project

The research team will not knowingly provide confidential farm data to unauthorized third parties.

---

## 10. Publications and Presentations

Research results derived from the farm data may be published in:

- Scientific journals
- Conference presentations
- Research reports
- Academic theses or dissertations
- Technical reports
- Other appropriate scientific communications

Published or presented results will normally be de-identified or aggregated so that individual farms cannot reasonably be identified.

The farm or farmer will not be specifically identified without written permission.

Where appropriate, publications will describe:

- The source and nature of the data
- Research methods
- Data limitations
- Analytical procedures
- Data-access restrictions

The farmer may request a summary of major research findings when appropriate.

---

## 11. Data Sharing and Future Research

The farmer may specify how the data may be reused after completion of the original research project.

Please select one option:

- [ ] De-identified data may be reused for future research related to agriculture, animal science, nutrition, food systems, sustainability, or data science.
- [ ] De-identified data may be reused only for research closely related to the original project.
- [ ] Data may only be used for the research project described in this agreement.
- [ ] Additional written permission must be obtained before any future research use.

Identifiable farm data will not be publicly released unless separate written permission is provided.

---

## 12. Data Retention

Research data will be retained for:

**[Number of years / Until specific date / According to institutional policy]**

During this period, the data will be stored under appropriate security and access controls.

At the end of the retention period, the data may be:

- [ ] Securely deleted
- [ ] Returned to the farmer
- [ ] Archived under restricted access
- [ ] Retained in de-identified form for future approved research
- [ ] Other: ______________________

Data already incorporated into published research, completed analyses, or formal research records may need to be retained according to institutional or research-integrity requirements.

---

## 13. Withdrawal

The farmer may request that the research team stop collecting additional farm data or stop using identifiable farm data by contacting the research team.

The research team will make reasonable efforts to honor such requests.

However, it may not always be possible to remove data that have already been:

- Fully de-identified
- Aggregated with other data
- Included in completed analyses
- Included in published research
- Incorporated into formally archived research records

The research team will explain any applicable limitations if a withdrawal request is made.

---

## 14. Responsibilities of the Research Team

The research team agrees to:

- Use the data only for approved purposes
- Protect confidential and identifiable information
- Follow appropriate data-security practices
- Maintain clear documentation of the data
- Apply FAIR data-management principles where appropriate
- Use appropriate analytical methods
- Report results accurately
- Describe relevant uncertainty and limitations
- Avoid identifying individual farms without permission
- Follow applicable institutional and research requirements

---

## 15. Responsibilities of the Farmer / Data Provider

The farmer or data provider agrees to:

- Provide the data voluntarily
- Identify any information that should be treated as confidential
- Inform the research team of known limitations in the data when possible
- Provide accurate information to the best of their knowledge
- Inform the research team if permission for future data collection is withdrawn
- Communicate any specific restrictions on data use or sharing

---

## 16. Intellectual Property and Research Outputs

Ownership of the original farm data remains with the farmer unless otherwise agreed in writing.

Research outputs produced by the research team, including:

- Statistical analyses
- Research code
- Analytical methods
- Predictive models
- Figures
- Scientific manuscripts
- Research frameworks

will be managed according to applicable institutional policies and any separate agreements between the parties.

Nothing in this agreement automatically transfers ownership of the farmer's original data to the research institution.

---

